    <section class="section">
      <div class="about">
        <img src="{{ 'img/jd-lighting-candles.jpg' | media }}" alt="JD Carlston lighting candles at a Love Lab dinner">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="lede">I’ve been reverse engineering love for as long as I can remember.</p>
          <p>I grew up on a farm on the edge of Salt Lake City, in a family of engineers and teachers. I learned early that you can take almost anything apart, find out why it works, and put it back together better. It took me longer to learn that people work the same way, and that the most important systems we live inside are the ones we can’t see.</p>
          <p>Today I help people see those systems and practice something new. I call myself a Human Engineer: I bring the rigor of twenty years in engineering leadership to the patterns that shape how we love, work and belong.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="label">Where my authority comes from</p>
      <h2>Four practices, <em>one thread</em></h2>
      <hr class="rule">
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="label">Engineering leader</p>
          <h3>Twenty years of building teams</h3>
          <p>Director of Product Engineering at Stack Overflow, where I led the Architecture Group and the teams behind the public platform. Before that, Senior Director at Pluralsight, overseeing architecture across an engineering organization of 300 to 500 people. I’ve hired, grown and led teams across the US, Europe, India, Australia and Brazil.</p>
          <div class="card__actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="{{ site.jdcarlston }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Engineering work ↗</a></div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="label">Coach</p>
          <h3>Since I was a teenager</h3>
          <p>I started coaching athletes young and never stopped coaching people. Since my teens I’ve trained peer supporters and facilitated groups for volunteer and philanthropic organizations, and I’m a crisis responder. I also studied directly with Dr. Robert Dee McDonald, co-founder of the <a href="https://teloscenter.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Telos Healing Center</a>, whose teaching shaped how I listen and how I build each process.</p>
          <div class="card__actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/level-up/">Level-Up Sessions</a></div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="label">Facilitator and speaker</p>
          <h3>Rooms that change things</h3>
          <p>I’ve spoken internationally and run hands-on workshops for engineers, community organizations and conferences, from domain-modeling workshops at Explore DDD to design work for a major metro water utility. Groups of ten to fifty, and people keep asking for more.</p>
          <div class="card__actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/love/">Practice Labs</a></div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="label">Event and ceremony maker</p>
          <h3>Hosting since my teens</h3>
          <p>I’ve been hosting gatherings since I was a teenager, and designing and holding rituals and ceremonies since 2012. I’ve led a volunteer sanctuary team at large arts gatherings for years, and planned everything from a 35-person wedding camp to candlelit lab dinners. I know how to plan a room so people feel safe enough to be real in it.</p>
          <div class="card__actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/about/">What Red Thread Labs does</a></div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="label">The method</p>
      <h2>Practice <em>Labs</em></h2>
      <hr class="rule">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Practice Labs is the method I use one-on-one and in groups. It works with how we hold our inner experience, and how changing that holding changes how we feel and act. My study with Dr. McDonald is a big part of it. From there I’ve woven in attachment science, nervous-system work, humanistic therapy and the engineering habit of testing one change at a time, into a methodology of my own.</p>
        <p>I keep my sources in the open: <a href="/lineage/">Research Roots</a> links the research behind every idea I use.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <p class="label">The artist</p>
      <h2>Under all of it, <em>one practice</em></h2>
      <hr class="rule">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>I’m an engineer and an artist, and I stopped treating those as two different things a long time ago. I paint, build fiber and sculpture pieces like the Windward Weave behind our home page, make music, and write poetry under the name Roz Madder.</p>
        <p>Under all of what I do is one practice: helping people, and the things we make together, become more whole. I don’t fix people. I help them see the patterns they live inside, and practice something new.</p>
        <p class="about__links"><a href="{{ site.maddercraft }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">My art, music and poetry at MadderCraft ↗</a> <a href="{{ site.linkedinPersonal }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

